# ------------------------------------------------------------ ingest_qualifying ------------------------------------------------------------

"""
Qualifying Results Ingestion — Jolpica API → Bronze Parquet
===========================================================
Endpoint : https://api.jolpi.ca/ergast/f1/{year}/{round}/qualifying.json
Coverage : 2010 – 2025 (all races, all drivers)

What this data contains:
    - Q1, Q2, Q3 lap times per driver per race weekend
    - Grid position (final starting position after penalties)
    - Gap to pole position (computed here)
    - Teammate gap (computed here)

Output:
    bronze/qualifying/
    ├── 2010_R01_qualifying.parquet
    ├── 2010_R02_qualifying.parquet
    ├── ...
    ├── 2025_R24_qualifying.parquet
    └── all_seasons_qualifying.parquet   ← combined master file

Note on data availability:
    - Q1/Q2/Q3 times available from ~2006 onwards
    - Some rounds only have Q1/Q2 if session was wet or cancelled
    - Pre-2003 format was different (single session) — not relevant for 2010+

Usage:
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_qualifying
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_qualifying --force
"""


# ------------------------------------------------------------ ingest_weather ------------------------------------------------------------


"""
Weather Data Ingestion — FastF1 → Bronze Parquet
================================================
Source   : FastF1 session.weather_data
Coverage : 2018 – 2025 (FastF1 weather data reliable from 2018)

Note on source:
    Weather data is NOT available from Jolpica/Ergast API.
    FastF1 provides weather readings recorded during the race session
    at ~1 minute intervals from official F1 timing feeds.

What this data contains:
    - Air temperature (°C)
    - Track temperature (°C)
    - Humidity (%)
    - Pressure (mbar)
    - Wind speed (m/s) and direction (degrees)
    - Rainfall (boolean)
    All recorded at session_time intervals during the race.

Output:
    bronze/weather/
    ├── 2018_R01_weather.parquet      (~80–120 rows per race)
    ├── 2018_R02_weather.parquet
    ├── ...
    ├── 2025_R24_weather.parquet
    └── all_seasons_weather.parquet   ← combined master file

Usage:
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_weather
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_weather --force
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_weather --year 2024
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_weather --year 2024 --round 1
"""

# ------------------------------------------------------------ ingest_laps ------------------------------------------------------------

"""
Lap Data Ingestion — Jolpica API → Bronze Parquet
=================================================
Endpoint : https://api.jolpi.ca/ergast/f1/{year}/{round}/laps.json
Coverage : 2010 – 2025, all races, all drivers, all laps

Response structure (nested):
    Race → Laps[lap_number] → Timings[driver] → position, time

So one API response looks like:
    Lap 1 → [verstappen pos=1 time=1:38.1, leclerc pos=2 time=1:38.5, ...]
    Lap 2 → [verstappen pos=1 time=1:32.4, leclerc pos=2 time=1:32.8, ...]

We flatten this to one row per driver per lap.

Output:
    bronze/laps/
    ├── 2010_R01_laps.parquet      (~1,200 rows)
    ├── 2010_R02_laps.parquet
    ├── ...
    ├── 2025_R24_laps.parquet
    └── all_seasons_laps.parquet   ← combined master (~432,000 rows)

Pagination:
    Each race has ~1,200 rows. With limit=100 that is ~12 API calls per race.
    With ~340 races total that is ~4,000 API calls for full ingestion.
    RATE_LIMIT_DELAY ensures we don't hammer the API.

Usage:
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_laps
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_laps --force
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_laps --year 2024          # single season
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_laps --year 2024 --round 1 # single race
"""

# ------------------------------------------------------------ ingest_pitstop ------------------------------------------------------------

"""
Pit Stops Ingestion — Jolpica API → Bronze Parquet
===================================================
Endpoint : https://api.jolpi.ca/ergast/f1/{year}/{round}/pitstops.json
Coverage : 2012 – 2025 (Jolpica pit stop data starts from 2012)

What this data contains:
    - Lap number when pit stop occurred
    - Duration of pit stop (time stationary in pit lane)
    - Local time of pit stop
    - Stop number per driver (1st stop, 2nd stop etc.)

Note on data availability:
    Jolpica pit stop data is only available from 2012 onwards.
    Pre-2012 seasons will be skipped with a warning.

Output:
    bronze/pit_stops/
    ├── 2012_R01_pit_stops.parquet
    ├── 2012_R02_pit_stops.parquet
    ├── ...
    ├── 2025_R24_pit_stops.parquet
    └── all_seasons_pit_stops.parquet   ← combined master file

Usage:
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_pit_stops
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_pit_stops --force
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_pit_stops --year 2024
    python -m src.ingestion.ingest_pit_stops --year 2024 --round 1
"""

# ------------------------------------------------------------ ingest_telemetry ------------------------------------------------------------

"""
Telemetry Ingestion — FastF1 → Bronze Parquet
=============================================
Source   : FastF1 session.laps.get_car_data() per lap per driver
Coverage : 2018 – 2025 (FastF1 telemetry reliable from 2018)

IMPORTANT — Why we aggregate and not store raw:
    Raw telemetry = 240 samples/sec × 5400 sec race × 20 drivers
                  = ~26 million rows per race
                  = ~8 billion rows for all seasons
    This is unworkable for PostgreSQL or even Parquet on a local machine.

    Solution: aggregate to one row per lap per driver.
    Aggregated = ~1,200 rows per race × 340 races = ~408,000 rows total.
    This preserves all meaningful signals for ML while being storage-safe.

What we extract per lap per driver:
    Speed      → avg, max, min
    Throttle   → avg %, full throttle % (time at 100%)
    Brake      → avg, heavy braking % (time braking hard)
    DRS        → % of lap with DRS open
    Gear       → avg gear, gear changes count
    RPM        → avg, max

Output:
    bronze/telemetry/
    ├── 2018_R01_telemetry.parquet     (~1,200 rows per race)
    ├── 2018_R02_telemetry.parquet
    ├── ...
    ├── 2025_R24_telemetry.parquet
    └── all_seasons_telemetry.parquet  ← combined master (~408,000 rows)
"""







